WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.720
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here, and today
I'm gonna tell you don't try to learn

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:11.970
the basics of the language that you are
trying to learn, and I'll tell you why.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:17.220
But remember, uh, if you enjoy
these videos, please subscribe.

00:00:17.225 --> 00:00:21.120
Click on the bell for notifications,
and if you follow me on a podcast

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.890
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:23.700
I do appreciate it.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.350
I'm gonna tell you, don't
try to study the basics.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.480
Don't try to master the basics.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:36.060
Don't even worry about the basics in a
language, especially at the beginning.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:37.470
Why do I say that?

00:00:38.339 --> 00:00:39.300
A number of reasons.

00:00:39.419 --> 00:00:44.250
Uh, first of all, because I have
found it impossible to master the

00:00:44.254 --> 00:00:50.040
basics of grammar, the basic, uh,
vocabulary, the basic anything, uh,

00:00:50.044 --> 00:00:54.989
if I deliberately try to do so, and
that's despite looking over explanations

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:58.410
and conjugation tables and all this
other stuff, it just doesn't work.

00:00:59.265 --> 00:01:03.375
The second thing, and I think this might
be more interesting, is something called

00:01:03.375 --> 00:01:06.675
the Natural Order of Language Acquisition.

00:01:07.035 --> 00:01:11.295
It's one of Steven Krashen's, sort of,
principles of language acquisition,

00:01:11.955 --> 00:01:18.585
and it says that there is a natural
order, uh, according to which we

00:01:18.585 --> 00:01:22.785
acquire the structures of a language.

00:01:23.685 --> 00:01:30.945
And if you were to, uh, Google the,
uh, Theory of Natural Order of Language

00:01:30.949 --> 00:01:35.265
Acquisition, you will find all kinds
of, uh, material on the subject.

00:01:35.324 --> 00:01:36.615
It's a bit controversial.

00:01:36.615 --> 00:01:38.895
You'll find people supporting the idea.

00:01:38.899 --> 00:01:41.085
You'll see people criticizing the idea.

00:01:41.625 --> 00:01:46.395
One of the criticism seems to be that
obviously if an English speaking person

00:01:46.395 --> 00:01:52.395
is learning French, then there may be
a natural order of acquisition for that

00:01:52.395 --> 00:01:54.495
English speaking person learning French.

00:01:54.575 --> 00:02:00.495
However, if a Spanish speaking person
learns French, then that natural

00:02:00.495 --> 00:02:04.995
order would be different because
obviously the Spanish speaking person

00:02:05.115 --> 00:02:10.905
is influenced by their familiarity
with, you know, Latin based languages.

00:02:11.455 --> 00:02:17.455
So assuming that that's true to me,
that doesn't in any way make the theory

00:02:17.455 --> 00:02:21.865
of natural acquisition less valid,
it just says that there is a natural

00:02:21.865 --> 00:02:26.040
order of acquisition, which may be
different depending on your native

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:29.670
language and depending on the language
you're trying to learn, but there is

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:34.739
a natural order, uh, you know, are
we gonna learn, you know, uh, if it's

00:02:34.739 --> 00:02:38.940
English, I go, or I'm going, which
one are we gonna start using earlier?

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:44.290
Uh, should the teacher teach you the, I'm
the continuous form first or in Spanish

00:02:44.929 --> 00:02:51.870
...
uh, before to, you know, the form
...uh, Is it important for the teacher

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:55.650
to sort of determine, you know, in
chapter one we will teach you this, and

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.299
in chapter two we will teach you that.

00:02:57.359 --> 00:03:02.040
And by the 20 chapters of this book,
you will have acquired all the basics

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:07.980
of the language or uh, and this is,
I think, Krashen's view and my view,

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:13.650
and that is that you will gradually
acquire these different aspects of the

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:18.704
language in an order that is more or
less the same for everyone, depending

00:03:18.704 --> 00:03:20.894
on your native language, depending
on the language you're learning,

00:03:21.195 --> 00:03:22.815
depending on some other factors as well.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:27.225
But there is a natural order and the
teacher and the instructions will

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.445
not influence that order so much.

00:03:30.015 --> 00:03:36.045
Uh, so, uh, but if you do a lot of
listening and reading, if you, you know,

00:03:36.625 --> 00:03:41.475
increase your level of comprehension, if
you increase your vocabulary, again, the

00:03:41.475 --> 00:03:44.385
vocabulary will be acquired naturally.

00:03:44.535 --> 00:03:48.994
Some words are going to appear more
often than others, so you'll acquire them

00:03:49.355 --> 00:03:51.825
earlier without any particular effort.

00:03:51.975 --> 00:03:54.585
You don't have to go and find
a list of the most common

00:03:54.585 --> 00:03:56.295
a hundred words, 500 words.

00:03:56.385 --> 00:04:00.675
They will show up and by dint of
showing up more often, probably

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:05.204
you'll acquire them earlier, but uh,
some of them may be resistant and

00:04:05.204 --> 00:04:07.935
there'll be some order whereby you
acquire the, acquire these words.

00:04:08.204 --> 00:04:11.954
You know, an example of how difficult it
is to acquire something that is usually

00:04:11.954 --> 00:04:16.779
taught very early is the third person
singular of the present tense in English.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.045
Everyone I think is taught that in English
it's, I go, you go, he or she goes, and

00:04:24.045 --> 00:04:26.415
then you go, or we go, you go, they go.

00:04:26.535 --> 00:04:30.855
So it's only the third person singular
in the present tense that has an S.

00:04:31.035 --> 00:04:33.075
It's not a difficult concept.

00:04:33.705 --> 00:04:37.215
Uh, and, and obviously when
you are reading in English, you

00:04:37.215 --> 00:04:40.995
don't even need to notice that
it's he goes rather than he go.

00:04:40.995 --> 00:04:41.775
It wouldn't bother you.

00:04:41.775 --> 00:04:44.115
It wouldn't affect your comprehension.

00:04:44.265 --> 00:04:50.025
However, when speaking, it's very
difficult for people to remember,

00:04:50.085 --> 00:04:54.070
you know, on the fly while speaking
English that that third person singular

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:58.000
of the present tense has to have an
S and, and if they're thinking about

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.419
it, they'll probably get it wrong,
but when it eventually becomes a

00:05:01.419 --> 00:05:03.700
habit, it'll just come out naturally.

00:05:03.789 --> 00:05:07.570
And of course, sometimes the third
person is hidden because it refers

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:09.460
to a house on the hill that on...

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:12.880
you know, and then when you finally
come to the verb, you've forgotten

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:16.510
whether that takes an S or doesn't take
an S if you're a non-native speaker.

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:21.650
Whereas if you're a native speaker,
somehow or other the, you know,

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:26.690
brain has developed habits so that
it will, in most cases hit the S.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.120
And it really doesn't matter if
you don't hit the S, the person

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:30.080
will still understand you.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:33.470
But it's just to illustrate that
there are certain habits in the

00:05:33.475 --> 00:05:35.810
language that take longer to acquire.

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:41.270
And so for that reason, when I see
people say, you know, first master

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:44.930
the basics, and then if I master
the basics, then I'll build on that.

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:49.200
And of course, everything
that we acquire in the, in the

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:50.610
language is more like jelly.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:51.810
We can't build on it.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:53.970
It's kind of this
amorphous thing that we...

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:57.420
fuzzy thing that we gradually
become more and more familiar with.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:03.180
A few more things slot in, things become
a little clearer and it's not obvious,

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:09.645
uh, which items, which grammatical
patterns, which words are gonna sock in

00:06:09.645 --> 00:06:12.495
first and which ones will come later.

00:06:12.855 --> 00:06:17.594
And it, it's not correction, it's
not because, uh, you know, if, if

00:06:17.594 --> 00:06:21.195
a child, even an English-speaking
child is, is, is used to, okay,

00:06:21.195 --> 00:06:24.915
the one pattern that the child will
pick up on is "ed" for past tense.

00:06:25.275 --> 00:06:28.105
So, uh, you know, uh, I eat, I eated.

00:06:28.605 --> 00:06:32.445
Uh, that's a normal thing to say that
shows the brain is, is, you know,

00:06:32.445 --> 00:06:34.245
thinking in terms of logical patterns.

00:06:34.245 --> 00:06:38.565
I eated, and even if the mother
doesn't correct the child, Eventually

00:06:38.565 --> 00:06:42.375
the child will start to say, I ate,
and that'll happen at a certain time.

00:06:42.435 --> 00:06:47.115
And it doesn't matter if the child spends
six months or a year saying I eat it,

00:06:47.414 --> 00:06:50.565
because eventually, I mean, they won't
be saying that when they're older, unless

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:54.790
they are surrounded by people who are
also saying, I eated, in which case that

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:56.830
will then become an ingrained habit.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:01.990
But I think it's, it's important to,
to uh, sort of, I think, de-emphasize

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:06.010
this idea of basics as if learning
a language is like building a house

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:09.970
and you lay the foundation and then
you start putting bricks in place, or

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:12.580
you know, wooden frame or whatever,
and you build up this structure.

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:13.810
That's not how it is.

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:17.140
It sort of comes at you and you
forget it and come back again.

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:21.880
And if you keep on listening and reading
and using the language and maybe hearing

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:25.090
the native speaker, what they say, which
is maybe a little different from what

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:31.180
you say and slowly but surely you will
develop proper habits in the language.

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:35.169
So, uh, you know, the idea that
we're gonna nail down the basics

00:07:35.169 --> 00:07:38.040
or learn the basics first, I
don't think it's realistic.

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:43.410
And I think a lot of people, uh, beat
themselves up because they will continue

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:46.810
to make mistakes in some of the most
basic things for a long, long time.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:49.980
I use the word basic, you know, in
that understanding we have like,

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:53.640
you know, people continue to not
putting an s on the third person

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:54.690
singular of the present tense.

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:57.120
It's basic maybe, but they
continue to get it wrong.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:00.990
And I do the same in languages that I
speak I get a lot of basic things wrong.

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:02.160
I continue to get them wrong.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.050
I can still communicate very well.

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:05.700
I understand what people are saying.

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:09.210
I can get my meaning across and I
continue to make the most basic mistakes.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:10.470
And we have to accept that.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:14.160
And that the only, and, and we can
review the rules and stuff and it

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.780
may or may not have some effect, but
ultimately it's only by continuing to

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:23.445
listen and read and speak and wanting
to, you know, wanting to pay attention

00:08:23.445 --> 00:08:24.615
to what's happening in the language.

00:08:24.705 --> 00:08:26.715
Slowly, slowly, slowly, we improve.

00:08:26.925 --> 00:08:30.735
So don't beat yourself up if
you can't master the basics,

00:08:31.185 --> 00:08:32.625
focus on enjoying the language.

00:08:33.044 --> 00:08:33.555
Thank you.

00:08:33.945 --> 00:08:36.405
And I'll leave you with a couple
of, uh, videos that I've done

00:08:36.405 --> 00:08:37.815
on the subject of basics before.

00:08:38.044 --> 00:08:38.655
Thanks.

00:08:38.924 --> 00:08:39.405
Bye for now.

